## What remains, in order

1. Native cumulative-write enforcement and bounded setup/scratch/report handling.
   The existing guard does not implement this; disk capacity and coordinator
   counters cannot substitute for the frozen requirement. A closed syscall
   profile with a parent listener precharging scalar writes is a possible
   implementation, not implemented or Linux-ABI-qualified in this step. Vector,
   async, copy/reflink, shared-mapping, truncate and other bypasses need closure.
   Data-byte accounting must not be described as physical disk/journal accounting.
2. Executable dispatcher output isolation and the lifecycle containment limitation
   recorded in v0.108. The new parent-side source dependency set still needs its
   complete authenticated successor closure; the frozen worker manifest is intact.
3. Bounded Linux qualification, then the existing dedicated-host/runtime/source
   admission, input backing/writer exclusion, actual resource/mount readback,
   combined budgets and exact real-import authorization.

These are remaining engineering/admission requirements, not newly invented
policy gates. No earlier gate is completed by synthetic tests alone. The
S3-backed pretrained checkpoint remains the intended base; its current remote
availability was not checked. Training and production stay disabled. A GPU is
not needed for the next local implementation work.
